<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=morse-code">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morse code</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1837</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as American Morse code, International Morse code</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Morse code is a method of telecommunication that encodes letters, numerals and punctuation as standard sequences of two signal durations, dots and dashes. Around 1837 the American artist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-morse">Samuel Morse</a> devised a code of numerals for the electrical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> he was developing with Joseph Henry and Alfred Vail; in 1840 Vail extended it to letters, giving the shortest signs to the commonest ones, and this code was first used in 1844. Friedrich Gerke's simpler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> alphabet of 1848, adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1851, led to the International Morse code of 1865, which also carried early radio communication from the 1890s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morse-code.jpg" alt="Morse code" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hp.Baumeler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morsetaste.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Early in the 19th century, European experimenters made progress with electrical signalling, using static electricity and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">Voltaic piles</a>. The simple on-or-off nature of the signals made it desirable to find a way of sending natural language with only electrical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulses">pulses</a> and the silence between them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-morse">Samuel Morse</a>'s first proposal for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> code was replaced by an alphabet-based code developed by Alfred Vail, which was used for commercial telegraphy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><p>Friedrich Gerke's 1848 proposal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> alphabet, did away with the different-length dashes and spaces of American Morse, leaving two elements, the dot and the dash. It was first used between Hamburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuxhaven">Cuxhaven</a>. Gerke changed nearly half the alphabet and all of the numerals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In International Morse, a dah lasts three times as long as a dit. The space between elements equals one dit, between letters three dits and between words seven. Vail designed his code so that the length of each symbol was roughly the inverse of the frequency of its letter in English, which is why E is a single dit. International Morse was standardised by the International Telegraph Convention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 17 May 1865, which formed the International Telegraph Union. Vail's version, known as American Morse or railroad code, remained in use only for land-line telegraphy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> the receiver's armature clicked as it marked a paper tape. Operators soon learned to translate the clicks directly into dots and dashes, which made the tape unnecessary. In the 1890s Morse began to be used extensively for early radio, before voice could be transmitted, and in the late 19th and early 20th centuries most high-speed international communication used it on telegraph lines, undersea cables and radio circuits. Radiotelegraphy was vital in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p>The final commercial Morse transmission in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> took place on 12 July 1999, signing off with Morse's 1844 message, What hath God wrought. Radio amateurs still use it, and radio navigation aids broadcast their identities in it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Morse code” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1837</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>United States</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Samuel Morse · Alfred Vail</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The alphabet that carries Morse's name was Alfred Vail's, who extended Morse's numerals-only design in 1840, and most of the present international code comes from Friedrich Gerke's revision of 1848.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">Electrical telegraph<span>1816</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=morse-code">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
